from author_proof_obligations import add
add('P150-0003','calculation stated without details','§2.5, equation (2.5.3): resolving-functor spectral terms','The two E2 pages of the resolving double complex equal the derived-functor expressions in (2.4.2).','Take cohomology first in the resolution direction using Proposition 2.5.1, and in the other direction using exactness of the resolving functor; check degree order and the signed total differential. Equality of terms is only the beginning of the comparison proved on the following pages.','resolving functor|hypercohomology|double complex')
add('P151-0001','proof supplied with comparison detail suppressed','Proposition 2.5.3: comparison of spectral functors','The two augmentation arrows through the auxiliary double complex give the canonical isomorphisms of the spectral sequences from E2 onward.','Verify the natural identifications for all four E2 arrows, independence of the chosen Cartan–Eilenberg resolution, and compatibility of the two induced abutment maps. The printed malformed II-arrow formulas are separately corrected in the ledger.','spectral functor|comparison|augmentation')
add('P151-0002','calculation within supplied proof','Proposition 2.5.3: column calculation','The vertical cohomology of the auxiliary totalized column is R^qF(K^p).','Apply the acyclic-resolution comparison to the resolution of the fixed term K^p; show finite diagonal totalization and the augmentation identify the relevant cohomology. This is a dependency inside the supplied proof, not an unproved independent theorem.','double complex|acyclic resolution')
add('P155-0001','stalkwise verification omitted','§3.1: stalks detect kernels and exactness','A map of sheaves of abelian groups is monic or epic precisely when every stalk map is, and stalks commute with kernels, cokernels, images and coimages.','Use exactness of filtered colimits of groups, equality of presheaf and sheafification stalks, and local representation/gluing of germs. For epimorphisms distinguish local lifting of sections from surjectivity of sections on each open set.','stalk exactness|sheafification|epimorphism')
add('P155-0002','definition-level verification','§3.1: module sheaves and additive structure','Compatible actions on sections are equivalent to an action by a sheaf-ring morphism into the internal endomorphism sheaf; module maps form an additive category.','Check compatibility on restrictions and germs, and construct addition and composition locally. Here the endomorphism object is internal sheaf Hom, not automatically the sheaf of all stalkwise endomorphism groups.','module sheaf|internal Hom|ring action')
add('P155-0003','examples and generator claim require details','Proposition 3.1.1: sheaf limits, AB5 and generators','Coproducts have coproduct stalks; arbitrary products need not have product stalks or preserve epimorphisms; extension-by-zero ring sheaves generate the module category.','Use sheafification for sums, sectionwise products for products, and filtered-colimit exactness for AB5. For the generator claim prove Hom(O_U,F)=F(U) by extension by zero. Supply an actual infinite-product lifting counterexample for the negative AB4* assertion; failure of a stalk-product comparison alone is not that counterexample.','AB4*|product sheaves|extension by zero|generator')
add('P156-0001','adjunction verification omitted','Proposition 3.1.2: Godement product construction','Maps into the product of point-supported sheaves are families of maps from the source stalks to the chosen modules.','Apply the stalk/right-adjoint adjunction for each point and the universal property of the product. Then injectivity follows because stalks are exact and products of injectives have the lifting property. Keep the chosen factor M_x distinct from the stalk of the full product sheaf.','Godement|skyscraper|injective sheaf|stalk')
add('P157-0001','left-exactness verification omitted','§3.2: sections with supports','Sections with support in a family closed under finite unions and closed subsets form a left-exact functor.','Check that a sum has support in the union of the supports, and that a section in the kernel lifts uniquely through a sheaf kernel without enlarging its support. The support axioms alone define the derived functor; later soft-resolution results require extra hypotheses.','sections with supports|left exact|support family')
add('P157-0002','naturality construction sketched','§3.2: inverse-image map on cohomology with supports','An exact inverse-image functor and a compatible map of support families induce the natural cohomology morphism.','Use (f inverse F)_x=F_f(x) to prove exactness, check the support inclusion for degree zero, and invoke the universal delta-functor property to extend the map. Injectivity need not be preserved by inverse image for this argument.','inverse image|cohomology with supports|naturality')
add('P158-0001','transitivity left to reader','§3.2: transitivity of pullback maps','Natural maps in cohomology compose in the same way as the underlying continuous maps with compatible supports.','Compare the two transformations in degree zero and invoke uniqueness of the extension from a universal delta functor, keeping the canonical inverse-image associativity isomorphism.','pullback|cohomology|transitivity')
add('P158-0002','proof supplied','§3.3: the acyclic-class criterion','The stated embedding, direct-summand and short-exact-sequence conditions make all objects of the selected class F-acyclic.','The supplied induction on the cycles of an injective resolution proves the claim; correct the duplicated degree-zero sequence as in the ledger and verify that the closure assumption applies at every induction step.','acyclic class|dimension shifting|injective resolution')
add('P159-0001','extension and softness facts asserted','§3.3: flasque and soft sheaves','The product-of-fibres construction yields flasque sheaves when the factors permit extension; on the stated paracompact spaces flasque sheaves are soft, and for a paracompactifying family they are Phi-soft.','For sets require nonempty factors or an appropriate enlargement, as recorded by the correction. Prove extension of a section from a closed paracompact subset to a neighborhood using a locally finite shrinking, then extend flasquely and cut off with a neighborhood in the support family. Keep the separation conventions needed by the shrinking argument explicit.','flasque|soft|paracompactifying|Godement')
add('P159-0002','main closure proof explicitly omitted','Proposition 3.3.1: acyclicity of flasque and Phi-soft sheaves','The flasque and Phi-soft classes satisfy the exactness and quotient conditions of the acyclic-class lemma.','For flasque sheaves prove surjectivity on sections of a quotient in a short exact sequence with flasque kernel by extending compatible local liftings. For Phi-soft sheaves use locally finite refinements and controlled closed neighborhoods to lift supported sections and prove quotient softness. The paracompactifying hypotheses enter these gluing steps.','flasque acyclic|soft acyclic|paracompactifying')
add('P159-0003','standard implication stated without proof','§3.3, Remark: fine sheaves are Phi-soft','Fine sheaves are Phi-soft for paracompactifying supports and hence acyclic for positive supported cohomology.','Choose a locally finite cover and the locally finite endomorphism partition subordinate to it, extend local representatives near the closed support, and sum the cut-off extensions. Verify local finiteness and support containment. The comparison to the earlier cohomology theory then needs the common effaceable resolution or uniqueness theorem.','fine sheaf|soft sheaf|partition of unity')
add('P160-0001','elementary example without full proof','§3.3: constant sheaves on irreducible spaces','A constant sheaf on an irreducible space is flasque; on a connected space, flasqueness of a constant sheaf with at least two elements forces every open subset to be connected.','Nonempty open subsets of an irreducible space are connected, so their constant-sheaf sections are constant and extend. Conversely a disconnected open subset supports two distinct locally constant values that cannot extend to a connected whole space. Acyclicity then follows from the flasque result.','irreducible|constant sheaf|flasque')
add('P161-0001','statement supported by preceding and subsequent arguments','Proposition 3.4.1: locally factorial varieties and projective bundles','In the Zariski topology of an irreducible locally factorial variety, H^i(O*) vanishes for i at least two, and a Zariski locally trivial PGL bundle lifts to a GL bundle.','Use the divisor sequence with the rational-function sheaf and the divisor sheaf flasque, then use the central-extension obstruction in H^2(O*). Do not replace Zariski local triviality by etale local triviality: that would introduce Brauer obstructions beyond this assertion.','locally factorial|divisor|PGL|Zariski torsor')
add('P161-0002','reference to omitted nonabelian obstruction theory','Proposition 3.4.1: H1 formulation','Surjectivity on nonabelian H^1 follows from vanishing of the central H^2 obstruction.','Define H^1 by torsors or nonabelian Cech cocycles, construct scalar obstruction cocycles from lifts of transition maps, and prove that a vanishing class permits corrected lifts satisfying the cocycle identity. Identify the topology and sheaf-level exactness.','nonabelian H1|central extension|obstruction')
add('P161-0003','local section asserted','Proposition 3.4.1: GL to PGL is locally split','The quotient map GL_n to PGL_n has Zariski local sections, so the central extension yields an epimorphism of the corresponding Zariski sheaves.','View PGL_n as the open determinant-nonzero locus of projective matrix space; on a chart with one matrix entry nonzero, normalize that entry to one. These charts give regular sections and prove the exact sheaf sequence.','PGL|GL|central extension|local section')
